\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Positive definiteness under elimination}
\date{2026-10-23}

\p{Let #{X,Y} be finite-dimensional real vector spaces with positive
definite inner products #{b_X,b_Y}. Give #{M=X\oplus Y} their
[orthogonal sum](fdip-001X), and let #{Q:M\to M} be
self-adjoint, with [components](fdip-001T) #{A,B,E,C}.
For a self-adjoint map #{T} on any of these spaces, write #{T>0}
when #{b(z,Tz)>0} for every nonzero #{z}.}

\p{If #{Q>0}, then #{C} is positive definite and invertible, and
#{S=A-BC^{-1}E} is positive definite and invertible.
Conversely, if #{C} is invertible, then
##{Q>0\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad C>0\ \text{and}\ S>0.}
}

\p{\citet{Appendix A.5.5, p. 650}{boyd2004convex} gives the
positive-definite Schur criterion. Here the eliminated summand is
placed second.}

\proof{
\p{The currying map of each inner product is injective. Indeed, if
#{b(z,u)=0} for every #{z}, take #{z=u}; positive definiteness gives
#{u=0}.
Finite dimension makes this map onto the dual, which has the same
dimension. Thus these forms are chosen perfect pairings, and the
[paired-congruence identity](fdip-001X) applies whenever
#{C} is invertible.}

\p{Assume #{Q>0}. For #{y\ne0}, #{\iota_Yy\ne0} and
##{b_Y(y,Cy)=b_M(\iota_Yy,Q\iota_Yy)>0.}
Hence #{C>0}. If #{Cy=0}, positivity forces #{y=0}; finite dimension
then turns injectivity into surjectivity, so #{C} is invertible.
For #{x\ne0}, the vector #{(x,-C^{-1}Ex)} is nonzero and congruence
gives
##{b_X(x,Sx)
 =b_M((x,-C^{-1}Ex),Q(x,-C^{-1}Ex))>0.}
Thus #{S>0}, and the same injectivity and finite-dimension argument
makes #{S} invertible.}

\p{Conversely, suppose #{C>0} and #{S>0}. For any nonzero
#{z=(x,y)}, the invertible map #{V(x,y)=(x,y+C^{-1}Ex)} has
#{Vz\ne0}. At least one of its two components is nonzero.
The corresponding term of
##{b_M(z,Qz)=b_X(x,Sx)+b_Y(y+C^{-1}Ex,C(y+C^{-1}Ex))}
is strictly positive, and the other is nonnegative. Hence #{Q>0}.
If a summand is zero, its positivity assertion is vacuous and its
unique endomorphism is invertible, so the same argument includes it.}
}

\p{This uses finite-dimensional real positivity in addition to the
ring-level factorization. It explains the positivity assumption in
[real effective precision](fdip-000D). It does not evaluate an integral
or determine a normalization factor; those require the separately
specified real measure and Gaussian law.}
